const BOUND_PATHWAY = "{\"id\":\"automatic-clean-slate-sealing-under-cpl-160-57\",\"label\":\"Automatic Clean Slate sealing under CPL 160.57\",\"summary\":\"An eligible misdemeanor conviction seals automatically 3 years after sentencing/release (8 years for an eligible felony), with no pending charges. OCA/DCJS process the back-catalog through 11/16/2027; verify status via a DCJS Record Review. If overdue, Sex offenses and any offense requiring sex-offender registration.\",\"sourceRef\":\"section:408-414; section:164-193; section:68-109; pathways:68-109; corpus-unit:25; corpus-unit:109; corpus-unit:41\",\"sourceEvidenceRefs\":[\"section:408-414\",\"section:164-193\",\"section:68-109\",\"pathways:68-109\",\"corpus-unit:25\",\"corpus-unit:109\",\"corpus-unit:41\"],\"ruleClauses\":[\"An eligible misdemeanor conviction seals automatically 3 years\",\"after sentencing/release (8 years for an eligible felony), with\",\"no pending charges. OCA/DCJS process the back-catalog through\",\"11/16/2027; verify status via a DCJS Record Review. If overdue,\",\"Sex offenses and any offense requiring sex-offender registration.\",\"Non-drug Class A felonies — including murder and other top-tier felonies. (Drug-related Class A felonies under Penal Law Article 220 CAN still qualify.)\",\"Cases where the person received a life sentence.\",\"Any person with pending criminal charges — ineligible while a charge is open.\",\"Federal convictions and out-of-state convictions — Clean Slate reaches NY State convictions only.\",\"Two-conviction / one-felony cap (160.59). No more than two convictions, and no more than one felony, may be sealed by petition.\",\"Mixed records. If a person has both eligible and ineligible convictions, the eligible ones may still be sealed; the ineligible ones remain.\",\"Sealed records still count. A sealed conviction still counts as a prior for sentence-enhancement in any future case (160.59(10)).\",\"The 160.59 “eligible offense” definition and the Clean Slate exclusions turn on the exact Penal\",\"Law article and class of each conviction (e.g., whether a Class A felony is a drug offense under\",\"Article 220). The agent must map each conviction to its Penal Law citation and class and verify\",\"against the current statute before declaring it eligible — the offense label alone is not enough.\",\"Note also the Brooklyn DA guidance that pre-2007 PL 265.02(4) weapon convictions are not\",\"The key Clean Slate nuance is the Class A felony split: non-drug Class A felonies are permanently\",\"excluded, but Article 220 drug Class A felonies can qualify. Check the article, not just the class.\",\"An open/pending charge disqualifies under both pathways — confirm the record is otherwise closed\",\"Five mechanisms, considered in routing order.\",\"Non-conviction sealing (CPL 160.50 / 160.55)\",\"When a case ends in the defendant's favor — dismissal, acquittal, declined prosecution, an adjournment in\",\"contemplation of dismissal (ACD) that is dismissed, or a vacated/overturned conviction — the record is\",\"SEALED automatically under CPL 160.50. Where the case ends in a non-criminal VIOLATION or traffic\",\"infraction, CPL 160.55 provides a more limited sealing. No application is needed; the court directs sealing at\",\"Marijuana destruction / expungement (MRTA)\",\"The one true erasure in New York. Convictions for unlawful possession of marijuana and fifth-degree criminal\"],\"triggerFields\":[\"case_outcome\",\"offense_level\",\"pending_cases\",\"offense_category\",\"disposition_date\",\"residency_or_location\"],\"caseOutcomes\":[\"convicted_misdemeanor\",\"convicted_other\",\"arrest_no_charge\",\"dismissed\",\"acquitted\",\"convicted_felony\"],\"automatic\":true,\"filingRequired\":false,\"routeType\":\"automatic\",\"suggestedResultCode\":\"guidance_only\",\"waitingRules\":[\"An eligible misdemeanor conviction seals automatically 3 years\",\"after sentencing/release (8 years for an eligible felony), with\",\"no more than ONE of which is a felony, after at least 10 years have passed since sentencing or release\",\"Clean Slate misdemeanor Seal (auto) 3 years From sentencing/release;\",\"Clean Slate felony (160.57) Seal (auto) 8 years From sentencing/release;\",\"Discretionary petition Seal 10 years ≤ 2 convictions, ≤ 1 felony;\",\"years misdemeanor / 8 years felony) but limited to its eligible set and still phasing in. The CPL 160.59 petition\",\"wait for the automatic system. For Clean Slate, any new conviction or pending charge during the waiting period\",\"Mechanism / matter Remedy Waiting period Notes Non-conviction (160.50) Seal None Sealed at favorable disposition Non-criminal violation Seal None Limited sealing at disposition (160.55) Marijuana possession (MRTA) Destroy/ None Automatic; OCA destruction on request expunge Drug conviction + treatment Conditional On program Reopens on new arrest (160.58) seal completion Clean Slate misdemeanor Seal (auto) 3 years From sentencing/release; (160.57) no pending charges Clean Slate felony (160.57) Seal (auto) 8 years From sentencing/release; no pending charges Discretionary petition Seal 10 years ≤ 2 convictions, ≤ 1 felony; (160.59) eligible offense The two conviction-sealing routes differ sharply in timing and effort.\",\"Clean Slate is automatic and faster (3 years misdemeanor / 8 years felony) but limited to its eligible set and still phasing in.\",\"For Clean Slate, any new conviction or pending charge during the waiting period restarts or blocks the clock.\"],\"exclusionRules\":[\"Sex offenses and any offense requiring sex-offender registration.\",\"Non-drug Class A felonies — including murder and other top-tier felonies. (Drug-related Class A felonies under Penal Law Article 220 CAN still qualify.)\",\"Cases where the person received a life sentence.\",\"Any person with pending criminal charges — ineligible while a charge is open.\",\"Federal convictions and out-of-state convictions — Clean Slate reaches NY State convictions only.\",\"Two-conviction / one-felony cap (160.59). No more than two convictions, and no more than one felony, may be sealed by petition.\",\"Mixed records. If a person has both eligible and ineligible convictions, the eligible ones may still be sealed; the ineligible ones remain.\",\"Sealed records still count. A sealed conviction still counts as a prior for sentence-enhancement in any future case (160.59(10)).\",\"The 160.59 “eligible offense” definition and the Clean Slate exclusions turn on the exact Penal\",\"Law article and class of each conviction (e.g., whether a Class A felony is a drug offense under\",\"Article 220). The agent must map each conviction to its Penal Law citation and class and verify\",\"against the current statute before declaring it eligible — the offense label alone is not enough.\",\"Note also the Brooklyn DA guidance that pre-2007 PL 265.02(4) weapon convictions are not\",\"The key Clean Slate nuance is the Class A felony split: non-drug Class A felonies are permanently\",\"excluded, but Article 220 drug Class A felonies can qualify. Check the article, not just the class.\",\"An open/pending charge disqualifies under both pathways — confirm the record is otherwise closed\",\"Sex offenses and any offense requiring sex-offender registration. • Non-drug Class A felonies — including murder and other top-tier felonies. (Drug-related Class A felonies under Penal Law Article 220 CAN still qualify.) • Cases where the person received a life sentence. • Any person with pending criminal charges — ineligible while a charge is open. • Federal convictions and out-of-state convictions — Clean Slate reaches NY State convictions only.\",\"If a person has both eligible and ineligible convictions, the eligible ones may still be sealed; the ineligible ones remain. • Sealed records still count.\"],\"frontendBranch\":{\"entryCondition\":\"Backend matched this jurisdiction-specific route after evaluating the cited source rules.\",\"missingFactsResult\":\"needs_more_info\",\"unmetTimingResult\":\"not_yet\",\"exclusionOrAmbiguityResult\":\"likely_not_eligible\",\"matchedResult\":\"guidance_only\",\"paymentAllowed\":false}}";
